    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-338555",
      "knowledge_point_id": "145bdcf2-b1d5-54a8-bd95-4b054d7a33f0",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Special Angles",
      "problem": "The solution to the equation $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is $x=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =3\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value of $x:$\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-{90}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-{90}^{∘}$ is not in the given domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={270}^{∘}\n$$\n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx={270}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSince the domain $0\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that \n$x={270}^{∘}$\nis the only solution.",
      "answer_fields": [
