    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-29005",
      "knowledge_point_id": "145bdcf2-b1d5-54a8-bd95-4b054d7a33f0",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Special Angles",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is not in the range $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required range, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2\\pi =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its minimum values at:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nSince the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is the only solution.",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
