    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-338585",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f9ccf6b5-e087-5f7b-88e3-3767efbdcc26",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Non-Special Angles",
      "problem": "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{4}{3},$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{3}{4})={(-48.590\\,377…)}^{∘}≈-{48.5904}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{48.5904}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={311.4096}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is ${x}_{1}={311.4096}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={48.5904}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={48.5904}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/d319d2ea403460a3b4d86c837a560fd5188476c425399145bb911e1414b0a6f5.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{48.5904}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{228.5904}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{228.6}^{∘},\\,x≈{311.4}^{∘}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
