    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-338798",
      "knowledge_point_id": "65e15de1-ee72-55ea-accb-1124f3a0ac0c",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant Under a Modified Domain",
      "problem": "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\sqrt{2}$ for $-2\\pi \\le x<0,$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we have to find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/012f0986293e2a4318dc68633a4ac0e91732d74300496427aaaa6635b417efe4.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ is not in the required domain $-2\\pi \\le x\\le 0.$ To get a solution that is inside the required domain, we subtract $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order,  the solutions are \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
