  "canonical_examples": [
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3533",
      "knowledge_point_id": "145bdcf2-b1d5-54a8-bd95-4b054d7a33f0",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Special Angles",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $2\\csc x+4=0$ for $0 < x <   2\\pi.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)} = -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain $0 < x <   2\\pi.$ To get a value inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\nx_1 = x + 2\\pi = -\\dfrac \\pi 6 + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the  fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Since $\\sin{x}<    0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}.$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4262",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f9ccf6b5-e087-5f7b-88e3-3767efbdcc26",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Non-Special Angles",
      "problem": "Find, in radians to $1$ decimal place, all of the solutions to the equation $\\dfrac{\\csc x}{5} -1 = 0$ for $0 \\leq x <              2\\pi.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{5}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})=0.201\\,357…≈0.201\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.201$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 0.201.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = 0.201.$ Since $\\sin{x} > 0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 0.201.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/8bcd1ea412f9235a330f37ee9059a023d0ce9e03e2c38388875dc0c12afebc1e.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -0.201 \\\\  & ≈2.941\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to one decimal place) are $x = 0.2, 2.9.$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3535",
      "knowledge_point_id": "65e15de1-ee72-55ea-accb-1124f3a0ac0c",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant Under a Modified Domain",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $2\\csc x + 4 = 0$ for $0 < x \\leq   \\dfrac{7\\pi}{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\csc {x} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{(-2)} = -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arcsin\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the required domain, we add $2\\pi.$\n\n$$\nx_1 = x+2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + 2\\pi = \\dfrac{11\\pi}6.\n$$\n\nSo, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the  fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of $\\theta_R = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Since $\\sin{x}<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle $\\theta_R =\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/85b7b73fa793153cfedb2ffdb6825edd0845f93dbb472404862a78476a56fc35.png)\n\nSince the second solution $x_2$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the remaining solutions in the given interval, we need to find angles that are coterminal with $x_1$ or $x_2.$ To do this, we add integer multiples of $2\\pi$ until all solutions are found:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} & ={x}_{1}+2\\pi =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{23\\pi }{6} \\\\ {x}_{4} & ={x}_{2}+2\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2\\pi =\\frac{19\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{23\\pi}{6}$ is outside the required domain, so we ignore it.\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6},\\dfrac{11\\pi}{6}, \\dfrac{19\\pi}{6}.$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4263",
      "knowledge_point_id": "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}$ for $-2\\pi \\leq x <           2\\pi.$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} =\\dfrac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe equation above has no solution because the sine function always satisfies $-1 \\leq \\sin{x} \\leq 1,$ and the value $\\dfrac{5}{2}$ is outside of this range.\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\csc{x} = \\dfrac{2}{5}$ has no solution.",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    }
